  "explanation": "In statistics, when we perform an experiment, we often want to understand if the result observed is due to some cause or if it happened by chance. The null hypothesis (often abbreviated as H0) serves as our starting assumption—it states that there is no effect or relationship. For example, it could be that taking more calcium has no effect on bone density. We test this idea with a significance level, which leads us to a p-value. The p-value represents the probability of seeing the data, or something more extreme, under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true. If the p-value is very low—typically below a chosen threshold like 0.05—we reject the null hypothesis, suggesting that the observed effect might not be due to chance alone."
